1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,700
 In the previous lesson, we built an agent that can reason over a single document and

2
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:10,640
 answer complex questions over it while maintaining memory.

3
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:15,040
 In this lesson, you will learn how to extend that agent to handle multiple documents in

4
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:17,480
 increasing degrees of complexity.

5
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:20,240
 Let's get coding.

6
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:24,520
 We'll start with a three-document use case, and then we'll expand to an 11-document use

7
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:25,520
 case.

8
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:35,600
 We'll set up our OpenAI key as well as import NASDAQ sync I/O.

9
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:40,500
 And then the first task is to set up our function calling agent over three papers.

10
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:46,480
 So we do this by combining the vector summary tools for each document into a list and passing

11
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:51,140
 it to the agent so that the agent actually has six tools in total.

12
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:55,000
 So we'll download three papers from ICLR 2024.

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,560
 This includes MetaGPT from last time, but this also includes LongLora, as well as SelfRag.

14
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:06,400
 Next, we'll convert each paper into a tool.

15
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:11,280
 So if you remember from lesson three, we have a helper function called getDocTools, which

16
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:17,160
 automatically builds both a vector index tool as well as summary index tool over a given

17
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:18,220
 paper.

18
00:01:18,220 --> 00:01:23,040
 And so the vector tool performs vector search and the summary tool performs summarization

19
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:25,400
 over the entire document.

20
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:29,600
 So for each paper, we get back both the vector tool and summary tool, and we put it into

21
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:36,160
 this overall dictionary, mapping each paper name to the vector tool and summary tool.

22
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:46,120
 Next, we'll simply get these tools in a flat list.

23
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:52,300
 We'll define 3.5 turbo from OpenAI as our LLM of choice.

24
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:56,520
 If we quickly take a look at the number of tools that are going to be passed to the agent,

25
00:01:56,520 --> 00:01:58,160
 we'll see that the number is six.

26
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:02,280
 That's because we have three papers and we have two tools for each paper, a vector and

27
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:04,560
 a summary tool.

28
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:10,700
 The next step is to construct our overall agent worker.

29
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:17,700
 And this agent worker includes the six tools as well as the LLM that we pass through.

30
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:22,760
 And now we're able to ask questions across these three documents or within a single document.

31
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:26,280
 For now, let's quickly ask a question about Long Laura.

32
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:42,280
 Tell me about the eval dataset used on Long Laura and then tell me about the eval results.

33
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:48,080
 We get back the answer that one of the eval datasets used is a Pg19 test split.

34
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:51,960
 And then we're able to look at the eval results for Long Laura models.

35
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:57,480
 The next question we can ask is give me a summary of both self-rag and Long Laura.

36
00:02:57,480 --> 00:03:01,800
 So this will actually allow us to do summarization across two papers.

37
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:07,520
 So first, we call the summary tool for self-rag with the input of its name, and we're able

38
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:13,240
 to get back the output describing what the paper is about.

39
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:18,000
 We see that the agent then calls the Long Laura summary tool with the input Long Laura and

40
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,560
 then we get back an overall summary of Long Laura.

41
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:27,360
 The final response is that we're able to get back both a summary of self-rag as well as

42
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:29,880
 Long Laura.

43
00:03:29,880 --> 00:03:34,360
 If you want to try out some queries on your own, you can try out any combination of these

44
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:39,760
 two papers or even three papers and ask for both summaries as well as specific information

45
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:44,080
 within the papers to see whether or not the agent is able to reason about the summary

46
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:47,360
 and vector tools for each document.

47
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:51,840
 Now let's expand into a more advanced use case.

48
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:57,400
 Here we'll actually have 11 iClear papers, and so we'll download 11 research papers

49
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:02,440
 from iClear 2024.

50
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:08,600
 This includes papers like MetaJPT, Long Laura, LoftQ, Sweetbench, Self-rag, as well as a

51
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:11,880
 few others.

52
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:17,960
 Similar to the previous section, we will now build a dictionary mapping each paper to its

53
00:04:17,960 --> 00:04:20,420
 vector and summary tool.

54
00:04:20,420 --> 00:04:24,920
 This section can also take a little bit of time since we need to process, index, and

55
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:30,180
 embed 11 documents.

56
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:34,440
 Now let's collapse these tools into a flat list.

57
00:04:34,440 --> 00:04:39,720
 This is the point at which we need a slightly more advanced agent and tool architecture.

58
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:46,540
 The issue is that let's say we try to index all 11 papers, which now includes 20 tools,

59
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:51,540
 or let's say we try to index 100 papers or 1,000 papers or more.

60
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:56,640
 Even though LLM context windows are getting longer, stuffing too many tool selections into

61
00:04:56,640 --> 00:04:59,880
 the LLM prompt leads to the following issues.

62
00:04:59,880 --> 00:05:03,880
 One is the tools may not all fit in the prompt, especially if your number of documents are

63
00:05:03,880 --> 00:05:08,240
 big and you're modeling each document as a separate tool or set of tools.

64
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:13,360
 Cost and latency will spike because you're increasing the number of tokens in your prompt,

65
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:16,040
 and also the LLM can actually get confused.

66
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:20,980
 The LLM may fail to pick the right tool when the number of choices is too large.

67
00:05:20,980 --> 00:05:27,840
 A solution here is that when the user asks a query, we actually perform retrieval augmentation,

68
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:31,780
 but not on the level of text, but actually on the level of tools.

69
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:36,120
 We first retrieve a small set of relevant tools and then feed the relevant tools to

70
00:05:36,120 --> 00:05:39,680
 the agent reasoning prompt instead of all the tools.

71
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:44,000
 This retrieval process is similar to the retrieval process used in RAG.

72
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,200
 Add its simplest, it can just be top-k vector search, but of course you can add all the

73
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:52,440
 advanced retrieval techniques you want to filter out the relevant set of results.

74
00:05:52,440 --> 00:05:58,000
 Our agents let you plug in a tool retriever that allows you to accomplish exactly this.

75
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,360
 So let's show you how to actually get this done.

76
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:03,420
 First we'll want to index the tools.

77
00:06:03,420 --> 00:06:08,680
 Llama Index already has extensive indexing capabilities over general text documents.

78
00:06:08,680 --> 00:06:12,920
 But since these tools are actually Python objects, we need a way to convert and serialize

79
00:06:12,920 --> 00:06:15,520
 these objects to a string representation in back.

80
00:06:15,520 --> 00:06:22,180
 This is solved through the object index abstraction in Llama Index.

81
00:06:22,180 --> 00:06:26,020
 So we'll define an object index and retriever over these tools.

82
00:06:26,020 --> 00:06:30,040
 We see that we import a vector sort index, which is our standard interface for indexing

83
00:06:30,040 --> 00:06:34,120
 text, but then we wrap this with the object index.

84
00:06:34,120 --> 00:06:39,680
 And to construct an object index, we see we directly plug in these Python tools as input

85
00:06:39,680 --> 00:06:42,640
 into the index.

86
00:06:42,640 --> 00:06:47,120
 You can retrieve from an object index through an object retriever.

87
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:51,400
 This will call the underlying retriever from the index and return the output directly as

88
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:53,120
 index.

89
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:59,240
 In this case, it will be tools.

90
00:06:59,240 --> 00:07:04,400
 Now that we've defined the object retriever, let's walk through a very simple example.

91
00:07:04,400 --> 00:07:09,920
 Let's ask, tell me about the eval data set used in meta GPT and also sweet bench.

92
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:19,200
 Now let's take a look at the first tool in this list.

93
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:24,120
 We see that we actually directly retrieved a set of tools and that the first tool is

94
00:07:24,120 --> 00:07:26,600
 the summary tool for meta GPT.

95
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:30,880
 If we take a look at the second tool, we see that this is a summary tool for an unrelated

96
00:07:30,880 --> 00:07:32,760
 paper to meta GPT and sweet bench.

97
00:07:32,760 --> 00:07:36,360
 So of course the quality of retrieval is still dependent on your embedding model.

98
00:07:36,360 --> 00:07:41,240
 However, we see that the last tool that's retrieved is indeed the summary tool for a

99
00:07:41,240 --> 00:07:42,640
 sweet bench.

100
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:45,840
 Now we are ready to set up our function calling agent.

101
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:49,720
 We note that the setup is pretty similar to the setup in the last lesson.

102
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:54,320
 However, just as an additional feature, we show that you can actually add a system prompt

103
00:07:54,320 --> 00:07:56,280
 to the agent if you want.

104
00:07:56,280 --> 00:07:57,280
 This is optional.

105
00:07:57,280 --> 00:08:01,240
 You don't need to specify this, but you can if you want just additional guidance, if you

106
00:08:01,240 --> 00:08:06,280
 want to prompt the agent to output things in a certain way, if you want it to take into

107
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:09,840
 account certain factors when it reasons over these tools.

108
00:08:09,840 --> 00:08:12,720
 And so this is an example of that.

109
00:08:12,720 --> 00:08:18,040
 Now let's try asking some comparison queries.

110
00:08:18,040 --> 00:08:34,480
 We ask, tell me about the eval dataset using meta GPT and compare it against sweet bench.

111
00:08:34,480 --> 00:08:39,640
 We see that it calls both the summary tool for meta GPT as well as the summary tool for

112
00:08:39,640 --> 00:08:40,640
 sweet bench.

113
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:44,920
 It's able to get back results for both.

114
00:08:44,920 --> 00:08:53,960
 And then it generates a final response here.

115
00:08:53,960 --> 00:08:59,080
 Now as a final example, let's compare and contrast the two Laura papers, long Laura

116
00:08:59,080 --> 00:09:09,040
 as well as loft Q and analyze the approach in each paper first.

117
00:09:09,040 --> 00:09:13,000
 We see that the agent is executing this query and the first step it takes is it takes this

118
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,900
 input task and actually retrieves a set of input tools to help it fulfill this task.

119
00:09:17,900 --> 00:09:22,320
 And so through the object retriever, the hope is that it actually retrieves long Laura and

120
00:09:22,320 --> 00:09:29,240
 loft Q query tools in order to help it fulfill its response.

121
00:09:29,240 --> 00:09:34,040
 So if we take a look at the intermediate outputs of the agent, we see that it is able to have

122
00:09:34,040 --> 00:09:38,640
 access to relevant tools from long Laura and also loft Q.

123
00:09:38,640 --> 00:09:43,000
 We see that first calls summary tool, long Laura with arguments approach and long Laura

124
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:46,400
 and you're able to get back a summary of the approach.

125
00:09:46,400 --> 00:09:54,340
 Similarly, you're able to get back the approach and loft Q by calling summary tool loft Q.

126
00:09:54,340 --> 00:09:59,760
 The final LLM response is able to compare these two approaches by comparing the responses

127
00:09:59,760 --> 00:10:05,440
 from these two tools and combining it to synthesize an answer that satisfies the user query.

128
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:07,400
 So that concludes our lesson.

129
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:11,720
 So now you should be equipped with the right tools to build agents not only over a single

130
00:10:11,720 --> 00:10:17,400
 document but also over multiple documents enabling you to build more general complex

131
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:21,120
 context augmented research assistance that can answer complex questions.

